=== SIGNATURE ===
move_rng(ad_or_rng, arrow, [distance])

=== COMMENT ===
Slides an address or range 'distance' cells (default 1) in one of the 8 arrow directions (glyph, numeric delta or letter code). A string/numeric address or range returns the moved range as a string in the same format ("B2:C10" ↖ -> "A1:B9"); a real range returns the CONTENTS at the destination. Clipped to the sheet: a partial overlap returns the part that fits; nothing left -> "oob".

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(ad_or_rng, arrow, [distance],
  LET(
    step_n, IF(ISOMITTED(distance), 1, distance),
    arr_norm, z_dir_to_arrow(arrow),
    d_row, SWITCH(arr_norm, "←", 0, "↖", -1, "↗", -1, "→", 0, "↙", 1, "↓", 1, "↘", 1, "↑", -1),
    d_col, SWITCH(arr_norm, "←", -1, "↖", -1, "↗", 1, "→", 1, "↙", -1, "↓", 0, "↘", 1, "↑", 0),
    one_val, ROWS(ad_or_rng) * COLUMNS(ad_or_rng) = 1,
    text_box, IF(one_val, IFERROR(z_ad_box(ad_or_rng), 0), 0),
    is_text, COUNT(text_box) = 4,
    is_ref, AND(NOT(is_text), ISREF(ad_or_rng)),
    box, IF(is_text, text_box,
         IF(is_ref, VSTACK(MIN(ROW(ad_or_rng)), MIN(COLUMN(ad_or_rng)), MAX(ROW(ad_or_rng)), MAX(COLUMN(ad_or_rng))), 0)),
    ok, AND(COUNT(box) = 4, ISNUMBER(d_row), ISNUMBER(step_n), IFERROR(step_n = INT(step_n), FALSE)),
    IF(NOT(ok), #VALUE!,
      LET(
        top_was, SUM(INDEX(box, 1)),
        lft_was, SUM(INDEX(box, 2)),
        top, MAX(1, top_was + d_row * step_n),
        lft, MAX(1, lft_was + d_col * step_n),
        bot, MIN(1048576, SUM(INDEX(box, 3)) + d_row * step_n),
        rgt, MIN(16384, SUM(INDEX(box, 4)) + d_col * step_n),
        IF(OR(bot < top, rgt < lft), "oob",
          IF(is_ref,
            OFFSET(ad_or_rng, top - top_was, lft - lft_was, bot - top + 1, rgt - lft + 1),
            LET(
              num_fmt, OR(ISNUMBER(ad_or_rng), NOT(REGEXTEST(ad_or_rng & "", "[A-Za-z]"))),
              corner_tl, IF(num_fmt, 1000000 * top + lft, ADDRESS(top, lft, 4)),
              corner_br, IF(num_fmt, 1000000 * bot + rgt, ADDRESS(bot, rgt, 4)),
              IF(AND(bot = top, rgt = lft), corner_tl, corner_tl & ":" & corner_br))))))
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Moves an address or range a number of cells in an arrow direction and tells you where it landed - or, for a real range, what is there.

Arguments:
  ad_or_rng  - what to move. Either
               * a string / numeric address or range ("B2", "B2:C10", 2000002, "2000002:10000003"; "$" and reversed corners are fine), typed in the formula or held in a cell - the result is the moved range as text in the same format; or
               * a real range reference (B2:C10) - the result is the CONTENTS of the destination cells, as an array (via OFFSET, so the formula is volatile like any OFFSET).
               A one-cell reference is read as its position unless the cell holds an address/range string, in which case that text is used (the same rule as neighbors_list / arrow_move).
  arrow      - one of the eight standard arrows (↑↓←→↖↗↙↘), its numeric-address delta (1, -1000000, ...) or a letter code (U D L R UL UR DL DR).
  [distance] - how many cells to move (default 1). An integer; negative moves the opposite way.

Clipping: the destination is cut to the sheet (A1:XFD1048576). If part of it falls off the sheet, the part that remains is returned ("B2:C10" moved ↖ by 2 would start at row 0 / column 0, so you get "A1:A8"). If none of it remains, the result is the text "oob".

A one-cell result is returned as a single address with no colon ("E2", or the numeric address as a number), consistent with expand_rng.

Returns:
  A range string / single address in the input's format, the destination's contents for a range reference, "oob" when nothing is left on the sheet, or #VALUE! for an unrecognised arrow, a non-integer distance, or an input that is neither a range nor an address.

Example:
  move_rng("B2:C10", "↖")        ->  "A1:B9"
  move_rng("B2:C10", "↖", 2)     ->  "A1:A8"          (first row and column fell off the sheet)
  move_rng("B2", "→", 3)         ->  "E2"
  move_rng("A1", "↑")            ->  "oob"
  move_rng(2000002, "↘")         ->  3000003          (numeric in, numeric out)
  move_rng(B2:C10, "↖")          ->  the values in A1:B9
